---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Massachusetts school spending explorer - Tim Shores"
  description="Per-pupil spending, enrollment, Chapter 70 aid, and revenue for every Massachusetts school district, and the general fund budgets of all 351 cities and towns, FY2009 to FY2025"
>
  <section id="explorer" class="explorer is-loading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Massachusetts · FY2009–FY<span data-latest>2025</span></p>
    <h1>School spending explorer</h1>
    <details class="intro" open>
    <summary>About this explorer</summary>
    <p class="lede">
      Why do our <span class="swap" data-districts="schools" data-towns="municipal services">schools</span> cost so
      much? Or are we not spending enough tax dollars on our
      <span class="swap" data-districts="schools" data-towns="towns">schools</span>? These are difficult questions to
      answer because it depends on your basis of comparison. Use this Explorer to discover the cost of your
      <span class="swap" data-districts="school" data-towns="town">school</span>.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Per-pupil spending, enrollment, and state aid for every Massachusetts school district, built on the data
      behind my report on
      <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-report-fy2025.pdf">per-pupil expenditure in Western Massachusetts</a>, with
      revenue by source for the regional districts. The Towns tab shows the general fund budgets of all 351 cities
      and towns, and the Commonwealth tab sets every district side by side.
    </p>
    <p class="lede"><em>You'll probably have a better experience exploring on a desktop than a mobile browser. I'll work on making that better.</em></p>
    </details>

    <div class="view-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explorer view">
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-districts" data-view="districts" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="true">School districts</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-towns" data-view="towns" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Towns</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-commonwealth" data-view="commonwealth" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Commonwealth</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-cap" data-view="cap" aria-controls="view-panel" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">The 82.5% cap</button>
    </div>

    <div id="view-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-districts">
    <form class="controls" onsubmit="return false" data-view-only="districts towns">
      <div class="control" data-view-only="districts">
        <span id="pick-district-label">District</span>
        <select id="pick-district" aria-labelledby="pick-district-label"></select>
      </div>
      <label class="control" data-view-only="districts">
        <span>Compare with</span>
        <select id="pick-compare">
          <option value="none">No comparison</option>
          <option value="cohort">Its cohort in its region</option>
          <option value="cohort-state">Its cohort statewide</option>
          <option value="region">Its region</option>
          <option value="state">Statewide</option>
          <option value="gateway">Gateway Cities</option>
          <option value="other">Another district or average</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <div class="control" id="vs-control" hidden>
        <span id="pick-vs-label">Comparison district</span>
        <select id="pick-vs" aria-labelledby="pick-vs-label"></select>
      </div>
      <div class="control" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
        <span id="pick-town-label">Town</span>
        <select id="pick-town" aria-labelledby="pick-town-label"></select>
      </div>
      <label class="control" data-view-only="towns" hidden>
        <span>Compare with</span>
        <select id="pick-town-compare">
          <option value="none">No comparison</option>
          <option value="peer">Its peer group in its region</option>
          <option value="peer-state">Its peer group statewide</option>
          <option value="region">Its region</option>
          <option value="state">Statewide</option>
          <option value="gateway">Gateway Cities</option>
          <option value="other">Another town or average</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <div class="control" id="town-vs-control" hidden>
        <span id="pick-town-vs-label">Comparison town or average</span>
        <select id="pick-town-vs" aria-labelledby="pick-town-vs-label"></select>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="control toggle">
        <legend>Dollars</legend>
        <div class="toggle-options">
          <label><input type="radio" name="dollars" value="nominal" checked /><span>Nominal</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="dollars" value="real" /><span>FY<span data-latest>2025</span> dollars</span></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <p class="controls-note" id="dollars-note"></p>
      <div class="relations" id="relations" hidden></div>
    </form>

    <header class="entity" data-view-only="districts towns">
      <h2 id="entity-name">Loading…</h2>
      <p id="entity-meta"></p>
    </header>

    <div id="tiles" class="tiles" data-view-only="districts towns"></div>

    <section id="cw-view" class="cw-view" data-view-only="commonwealth" hidden>
      <p class="cw-intro">
        Every Massachusetts school district side by side, two measures at a time. Open a plot to choose the
        measures, the year, and which districts stand out, and to see which factors line up most closely with
        spending.
      </p>
      <div id="cw-root"></div>
    </section>

    <section id="cap-view" class="cap-view" data-view-only="cap" hidden>
      <h2 class="cap-title">The 82.5% cap</h2>
      <p class="cap-lede">
        Every Massachusetts school district gets at least 17.5% of its foundation budget from the state, however
        wealthy its towns. That guarantee comes from a cap in the Chapter 70 formula: no town's target local
        contribution can be more than 82.5% of its foundation budget.
        <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/">MASS Promise to Invest</a>, a statewide campaign for school funding,
        argues that the cap costs everyone else. The formula asks every town for the same share of its wealth and sets
        that share so the targets add up to a fixed total, so holding down the wealthiest towns' targets raises the
        share for all the rest. In the campaign's words, “To fund the reduced rate as a result of the cap for the
        wealthy districts, the lowest income districts essentially pay an increased amount.” Montague resident Michael
        Naughton worked out every town's FY2026 target with and without the cap from DESE's formula spreadsheet, and
        the campaign posted
        <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/fy26-chapter-70-cap-comparisons.xlsx">his workbook</a>
        on <a href="https://masspromise2invest.org/information-2/">its information page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="cap-lede">
        I like this work, and I'm curious about it. The arithmetic holds up in DESE's own spreadsheets, and the size of
        it surprised me. This tab is where I'm building tools to understand what it means town by town: try another
        cap, look up a town, and see who pays more and who pays less.
      </p>
      <div id="cap-root"></div>
      <section class="cap-section cap-questions">
        <h3>Questions I'm still working through</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Targets aren't bills.</strong> When a town's required contribution is above its target, it drops to
            the target. When it's below, it grows by the town's municipal revenue growth factor, plus 1 or 2 percent
            more if it's far behind. Would lifting the cap cut many towns' requirements at once and raise the wealthiest
            towns' over years? What happens in between?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where would the money go?</strong> Chapter 70 aid starts each year from the year before. A district
            whose aid and required contribution fall short of its foundation budget gets foundation aid to close the
            gap; the rest get a minimum increase, $160 per pupil in FY2027. How much would a higher target lower a
            wealthy town's aid, and what would lower targets elsewhere cost the state?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Who counts as lower income?</strong> The formula counts the income on residents' state tax returns.
            Amherst, where students count as residents, has one of the lowest incomes per resident in the state, yet it
            is at the cap in both years: with the cap's percentages, its property and income yield more than 82.5% of
            its foundation budget. How many towns would a different measure of income move?
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Regional districts.</strong> A town's contribution is divided among its school districts by each
            one's share of the foundation budget for the town's pupils, so the cap reaches regional districts through
            their member towns. How does it add up district by district?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <p class="cap-method">
        <strong>Sources and method.</strong> Each town's figures come from DESE's complete formula spreadsheets for
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2026/chapter-2026.xlsm">FY2026</a> and
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2027/chapter-2027.xlsm">FY2027</a>, the Townwide
        Contributions sheet (<a href="/mass-school-spending/data/towns-chapter70-targets.csv" download>CSV</a>). For
        another cap, or none, I keep the formula's rule that targets add up to 59% of all foundation budgets and scale
        both percentages by the one factor that makes them add up; each town's target is then the smaller of its scaled
        yield and the new cap. For FY2026 this matches Naughton's targets with a 100% cap to the dollar, and with no cap
        to within a hundredth of a percent. Per-resident figures use the Census Bureau's July 2024 population
        estimates; income per resident divides the formula's income by the estimate for July of that year.
      </p>
    </section>

